CAT = "phpsyDevLife"; SEG = "DVL"
Q = [
("A senior high student takes the nursing course her parents chose without ever exploring other options. Marcia would call her identity status:",
 "Foreclosure", ["Moratorium", "Diffusion", "Achievement"],
 "Foreclosure is commitment without exploration, often to the family's choice. Achievement is commitment after exploration; moratorium is exploration without commitment yet."),
("A 15-year-old is sure that everyone at the mall will notice a small pimple on his chin. Elkind called this:",
 "Imaginary audience", ["Personal fable", "Moral realism", "Hypothetical reasoning"],
 "The imaginary audience is the teen's belief that others are as focused on him as he is. The personal fable is the belief that his life and feelings are unique."),
("A teenager believes no one has ever loved as deeply as she does and that bad things will not happen to her. Elkind called this:",
 "Personal fable", ["Imaginary audience", "Identity diffusion", "Moral realism"],
 "The personal fable includes feeling unique and invulnerable, which can feed risk taking. The imaginary audience is the sense of being watched."),
("Arnett's term for the years from about 18 to 25, when many young people explore work and relationships before settling:",
 "Emerging adulthood", ["Late adolescence", "Middle adulthood", "Psychosocial moratorium"],
 "Arnett proposed emerging adulthood as a distinct period in industrialized societies, marked by identity exploration and instability. It is not simply late adolescence."),
("In Erikson's theory, the crisis of young adulthood is:",
 "Intimacy vs. isolation", ["Identity vs. role confusion", "Industry vs. inferiority", "Trust vs. mistrust"],
 "Young adults seek close, committed relationships or risk isolation. Identity versus role confusion comes earlier, in adolescence."),
("In Sternberg's triangular theory, love with intimacy and commitment but little passion is:",
 "Companionate love", ["Romantic love", "Fatuous love", "Empty love"],
 "Companionate love combines intimacy and commitment, as in many long marriages. Romantic love is intimacy plus passion; empty love is commitment alone."),
("Which kind of intelligence tends to hold steady or grow into later adulthood, as with vocabulary and general knowledge?",
 "Crystallized intelligence", ["Fluid intelligence", "Perceptual processing speed", "Working memory"],
 "Horn and Cattell described crystallized intelligence as accumulated knowledge, which keeps rising or stays level. Fluid reasoning and speed decline earlier."),
("The gradual loss of hearing for high-pitched sounds that comes with age is:",
 "Presbycusis", ["Presbyopia", "Tinnitus", "Glaucoma"],
 "Presbycusis is age-related hearing loss, worst for high frequencies. Presbyopia is the age-related loss of near focus in the eye."),
("A dying woman shouts at her nurses and keeps asking, \"Why did this happen to me?\" Kubler-Ross would place her in which stage?",
 "Anger", ["Denial", "Bargaining", "Acceptance"],
 "Anger often follows denial, with resentment aimed at family, staff or God. Kubler-Ross noted the stages need not come in a fixed order."),
("Which theory holds that older adults are most satisfied when they stay busy with roles, friends and community activities?",
 "Activity theory", ["Disengagement theory", "Wear-and-tear theory", "Programmed theory"],
 "Activity theory says keeping up roles and activity supports well-being in old age. Disengagement theory said withdrawal from roles is natural and mutual."),
("Carstensen's socioemotional selectivity theory predicts that, as time feels shorter, older adults:",
 "Prefer fewer, closer ties", ["Seek many new friends", "Withdraw from all ties", "Value novelty over closeness"],
 "As the future feels limited, people put emotional meaning first and keep their closest relationships. They prune peripheral ties rather than all ties."),
("Levinson's term for the period around age 40 when adults reappraise the life structure they have built:",
 "Midlife transition", ["Early adult transition", "Age 30 transition", "Late adult transition"],
 "Levinson described the midlife transition, roughly 40 to 45, as a time to question one's dreams and commitments. The age 30 transition comes a decade earlier."),
("An aging pianist plays fewer pieces, practices them more and slows before fast passages so they sound quicker. Baltes called this:",
 "Selective optimization with compensation", ["Gradual disengagement from all valued roles", "Continuity of earlier habits", "Terminal decline in ability"],
 "Baltes's model says older adults select fewer goals, optimize the skills they keep and compensate for losses. The pianist is still fully engaged, so this is not disengagement."),
("Middle-aged adults who care for their own children and their aging parents at the same time are called the:",
 "Sandwich generation", ["Empty nest generation", "Boomerang generation", "Silent generation"],
 "The sandwich generation is squeezed between two sets of dependents. Boomerang children are adult children who move back home."),
("Research suggests puberty that comes much earlier than peers carries the most risk of emotional problems for:",
 "Early-maturing girls", ["Early-maturing boys", "Late-maturing girls and boys", "On-time boys"],
 "Early-maturing girls show more depression, body dissatisfaction and contact with older peers. Early maturation in boys is often linked to more popularity."),
("The permanent end of menstruation, confirmed after 12 months without a period, is called:",
 "Menopause", ["Andropause", "Menarche", "Osteoporosis"],
 "Menopause is usually dated a year after the last period, commonly around age 50. Menarche is the first period."),
("Peck said a retired teacher must find worth in roles other than her former job. He called this task:",
 "Ego differentiation", ["Body transcendence", "Ego transcendence", "Role diffusion"],
 "Ego differentiation versus work-role preoccupation is Peck's first task of old age. Body transcendence is about rising above physical decline."),
("In Kohlberg's theory, postconventional moral reasoning rests mainly on:",
 "Universal ethical principles", ["Avoiding punishment", "Approval of others", "Following the law as written"],
 "Postconventional reasoners judge laws by principles such as justice and human rights. Following the law for its own sake is conventional stage 4."),
("Human cells divide only a limited number of times before stopping. This supports which theory of aging?",
 "Cellular clock theory", ["Wear-and-tear theory", "Free radical theory", "Rate-of-living theory"],
 "The Hayflick limit suggests a built-in limit to cell division, a programmed or cellular clock view of aging. Wear-and-tear theory blames accumulated damage from use."),
("Many Filipino adult children support their aging parents as repayment for being raised. This reflects the value of:",
 "Utang na loob", ["Bahala na", "Hiya", "Pakikiramdam"],
 "Utang na loob is a debt of gratitude that binds children to care for parents. Bahala na is leaving matters to fate; hiya is a sense of shame or propriety."),
]
